00:02:10.517 --> 00:02:11.600
and create new businesses.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.260
One of my favorite examples
is in the airline industry.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.139
So all those Rolls
Royce engines that you

00:02:17.139 --> 00:02:19.180
see on airlines as you're
flying around the world

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.910
and looking out the window and
see the little RR letters--

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.290
they're not actually
owned by the airlines.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.085
They're actually leased to
the airlines by Rolls Royce.

00:02:28.085 --> 00:02:30.460
And, you know, the reason for
that is really interesting.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.870
Rolls Royce realized that if
they can manage all of the data

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.510
and collect all the
telemetry from those engines,

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.990
do rich analytics on it,
do machine learning on it,

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:44.110
they can actually deliver
a much better experience

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:45.820
for their customers,
the airlines,

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:48.430
by ensuring that the engines
are better maintained

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.680
and that the airplanes
themselves remain up in the air

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:51.890
much longer.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.940
So they gather huge amounts of
data, huge amounts of telemetry

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.080
from these engines.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.510
They capture it, they
run analytics on it,

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.160
they do predictive
maintenance on it.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.360
They don't, you
know, strip engines

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.160
down on regular
schedules, go check them,

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.910
and then put them all
back together again.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:07.360
They're much more
able to tell when

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.850
they need to bring an engine
in for repair and maintenance.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.920
All sorts of industries
are being transformed

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.190
by this amazing ability to
gather huge amounts of data

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:21.260
and do analytics on it.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.100
So all industries,
many use cases,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.065
amazing transformations
happening.

00:03:27.065 --> 00:03:29.440
Let's talk a little bit,
though, about what that actually

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.290
means in reality when you are,
sort of, feet in the ground,

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:34.150
working in an
enterprise, and thinking

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.250
about I've got
some infrastructure

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:39.400
and now I need to think
about using the cloud

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.310
to take advantage of some of
the capabilities that it offers.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.940
And I sort think of, like,
three main stages of journeys

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.010
that customers embark on.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:49.889
Sometimes they're doing
these in parallel.

00:03:49.889 --> 00:03:52.180
The first part is probably
like four or five years ago,

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.770
where organizations
were thinking, OK,

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.396
I've got my on-premises
infrastructure.

00:03:55.396 --> 00:03:56.770
What I'm going to
do is I'm going

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.742
to use the cloud for
backup, for archival.

00:03:58.742 --> 00:04:00.700
I'll continue to use it
through the application

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.020
that I have today.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.546
I'm going to reduce
my operational cost.

00:04:04.546 --> 00:04:05.920
I'm a bit worried
about security,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.240
I'm a bit worried
about networking,

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.240
but yeah, I'll sort of
dip my toe into the cloud

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.250
and I'll start to archive
and put some of my cold data

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.170
up in the cloud.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.079
Or maybe I'm going to burst some
of my compute into the cloud

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:20.417
because I don't want to buy
some new capital infrastructure.

00:04:20.417 --> 00:04:22.125
And so key areas of
GCP that matter there

00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:25.060
are things like compute
storage, networking.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.660
Security is very big-- ensuring
you have the networking

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.382
capabilities to
set up the security

00:04:30.382 --> 00:04:32.590
that you need between your
on-premises infrastructure

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:33.827
and the cloud.

00:04:33.827 --> 00:04:35.410
Sort of the second
step in the journey

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:38.260
is when organizations start
to think about actually

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:41.114
lifting and shifting significant
parts of their infrastructure

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:42.280
and running it in the cloud.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.110
So control is in the cloud.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.750
And here, you're
thinking about running

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.830
a much larger-scale
enterprise applications,

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.560
like scale-up databases,
running them in VMs.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.720
You heard some of the
great announcements

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.827
this week about things like
having HANA running now in GCP,

00:04:56.827 --> 00:04:58.660
and being able to just
really lift and shift

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.090
your infrastructure from
on-premise to the cloud,

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:04.030
and really start to think about
the cloud as the primary place

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.760
for running key parts
of your infrastructure.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:08.896
Often that infrastructure
might look still

00:05:08.896 --> 00:05:10.270
quite a lot like
what you've been

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:11.644
used to running
on premises, it's

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:16.100
just you're choosing to run and
operate it in a different way.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.940
Also here, you're starting to
think about running much more

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.570
compute in the cloud.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:21.550
A great example--

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:23.920
I worked with a
company that wanted

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.380
to do risk analysis for
all of their trade analysis

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:27.424
during the day.

00:05:27.424 --> 00:05:29.590
So they'd gather large
amounts of data and telemetry

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:34.270
from their financial traders,
and for reporting purposes

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:37.370
that have to sort of understand
the risk in their portfolio.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.880
So every evening, they'd run
large amounts of compute over

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.790
that data to understand their
risk portfolio to report

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:43.840
for compliance purposes.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:45.631
And they were literally
faced with a choice

00:05:45.631 --> 00:05:49.684
of should I buy a new physical
data center and provision it

00:05:49.684 --> 00:05:51.100
with all the
hardware that I need,

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.860
or should I leverage the cloud
for the large amount of burst

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.179
compute that it can
give me over that data?

00:05:56.179 --> 00:05:57.970
And as you start to
move in this direction,

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.511
you're starting to look for much
more managed infrastructure.

00:06:00.511 --> 00:06:03.790
You don't want to be
managing one or two VMs--

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:05.320
OK, not too bad.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.780
Setting up clusters
of thousands of VMs

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.510
for either compute or
for Hadoop is really

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:12.472
not a business that you
want to be in anymore,

00:06:12.472 --> 00:06:14.680
and so there's a set of
managed capabilities that you

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.350
see in GCP now that let
you manage and operate

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.244
large amounts of compute and
analytics and data at scale.

00:06:21.244 --> 00:06:22.660
And then there's
applications that

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:24.370
are just getting built net new.

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:27.169
So for cloud-native companies,
this is where they often start.

00:06:27.169 --> 00:06:28.960
They don't have any
existing infrastructure

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.910
so they just start new.

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:32.550
And you get new
classes of applications

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.510
here for they, like, app
dev, web, mobile, et cetera,

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:39.610
but also new applications
for data in analytics.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.270
And then you'll see a great
set of capabilities here

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:45.130
from, like, BigQuery,
machine learning, App Engine

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.760
to help you sort of build net
new capabilities in the cloud.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:50.454
So these two slides--

00:06:50.454 --> 00:06:52.120
I just want to set
up a lot of use cases

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.370
that we see that are very
exciting to do in the cloud,

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.980
and there's a lot of different
journeys to get there.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:58.521
What I'll talk a
little bit about now

00:06:58.521 --> 00:07:02.440
is a bit more of what's been
the evolution on the storage

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:06.350
and the data side.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:09.400
So if we think a little bit
about the evolution of storage,

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:14.110
you know, you start with the
basic direct attached storage.

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:16.330
So physical disks attached
to a physical computer

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:17.650
sitting under a desk, right?

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:21.970
That's kind of the
classic initial wave

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:27.220
that we bound compute
storage and storage together.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.320
That has steadily
evolved in enterprises

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:33.250
now to bringing in SANs and NAS.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:35.740
And the key thing there
is this observation

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:38.770
that hey, look, if we
separate the compute

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.890
from the storage with
a network in between,

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:43.270
then that gives us flexibility.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.420
We can better control how
we provision our storage.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:49.660
We can better control how
we provision our compute.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:54.320
We can set different policies
on performance, et cetera.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.650
Let me ask-- how many of
you are today running SANs?

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:00.500
All right, a handful.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:01.020
OK.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:02.560
How many of you are running NAS?

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:04.680
So filers-- shed
filers in some way?

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:05.400
Yeah, OK.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.704
A few more hands there, right?

00:08:06.704 --> 00:08:08.370
So these are, like,
core building blocks

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:12.580
of enterprise
infrastructure that people

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:14.426
are used to using today.

00:08:14.426 --> 00:08:16.050
And if you look at
what we have in GCP,

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.260
we have a set of capabilities
there to enable that.

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:22.020
So if you're just running
VMs with disks attached,

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.950
then we have Google Compute
Engine together with local SSD

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:29.520
or persistent desk, which
gives you a way to create a VM

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:32.159
and attach, effectively,
block storage to that.

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:34.590
Local SSD is
similar to DAS where

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.600
it's sort of local
to the machine,

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:43.230
whereas PD is more the data
is coming over the network.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:47.760
How many people are running
on-premise object stores?

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.400
Wow, you've got the
whole lot, right?

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.650
Yeah, so object
storage is something

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.521
that came about a little
bit more recently.

00:08:54.521 --> 00:08:56.520
We don't actually see a
lot of people running it

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.460
because most people now do their
object storage in the cloud,

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:01.980
but this is also
another way that people

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:06.390
have chosen to store typically
large, unstructured blobs

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:06.960
of data.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.619
And we have a product, Google
Cloud Storage, for that.

00:09:09.619 --> 00:09:11.160
So this has sort of
been a little bit

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.820
of the evolution of, kind of,
the core storage technology,

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.160
and the set of products that
we have to sort of meet you

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:20.760
where you are in evolving
those technologies

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.740
and applications to the cloud.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:26.370
OK, let's switch over to
databases and analytics.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:30.360
So, you know, I used to work
on a database product, a very

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.250
popular database
product, and I kind of

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.010
joked to the very
senior people who

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.562
I worked with who helped
create and build that product--

00:09:37.562 --> 00:09:39.270
man, you guys had it
kind of easy, right?

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:42.030
Because everybody knows what a
traditional database is, right?

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:45.420
So for 30, 40 years, the
traditional database industry

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:46.680
was kind of easy--

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:47.920
"easy."

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.370
You had a traditional
database, you'd

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:53.580
could capture your transactional
data against the database

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:57.570
if you wanted to do reporting on
it you would use ETL to extract

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:00.690
data from the database,
transform it, and then load it

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.469
into a data warehouse.

00:10:02.469 --> 00:10:04.510
You knew the questions
you wanted to answer like,

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:08.430
hey, what's the sales
by region or sales

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.760
by rep that I want to
look at every week?

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.830
So you knew that as you're
pulling the data out,

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:15.913
you could transform it
into a different, you know,

00:10:15.913 --> 00:10:20.310
snowflake schema,
whatever schema you chose,

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:22.750
and then run your query and
reporting against that on,

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:27.030
like, a regularly scheduled
monthly, weekly basis, right?

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:30.326
And this was the world of data
and analytics for, you know,

00:10:30.326 --> 00:10:32.150
20, 30 years.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:35.040
The pattern was very,
very well-established.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.900
Who knows this pattern?

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:37.410
All right.

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:38.640
OK, most hands so far.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:40.620
Everyone, this is a really
well-established and

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:43.530
well-understood
enterprise pattern.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:48.480
And Oracle, IBM, Microsoft
had great databases,

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:50.070
also great data warehouses.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.710
Teradata and others provided
that infrastructure.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:57.930
And then about 10 years ago,
kind of, all hell broke loose.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:00.480
You know, there was
so much innovation

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:01.720
happening at the same time.

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:03.240
So big data started
to come along.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:04.111
Hadoop came along.

00:11:04.111 --> 00:11:06.610
The Hadoop vendors would say,
throw away our data warehouse.

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:07.693
You don't need it anymore.

00:11:07.693 --> 00:11:10.590
Put all your data into Hadoop.

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:12.720
You need a place to
capture gigantic amounts

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.190
of unstructured data.

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:16.292
Data warehouses are not
cost-effective for that,

00:11:16.292 --> 00:11:17.250
so where do you put it?

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.860
So Hadoop was a great place
to capture huge amounts

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:21.690
of unstructured data.

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:25.230
Then there's specialist forms of
stores that started to emerge.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:28.890
Well, what happens if I don't
have really structured data

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:31.080
that I need to perform
particular transactions on,

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:32.220
and I can make compromises?

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.997
What happens if I want to
capture really large amounts

00:11:35.997 --> 00:11:37.830
of data that I don't
really know necessarily

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:39.996
what the columns are that
are going to be over time?

00:11:39.996 --> 00:11:42.240
Like IoT is a great scenario.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:46.380
Cell towers are all always
generating cell data records

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:47.850
in terms of all the calls.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:51.150
Different versions of the
software, different versions

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:52.710
of the vendors,
different versions

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:54.450
of the software on
the cell towers all

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.780
mean that there's no
single, well-formed piece

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:00.360
of transactional data
that's getting produced.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:01.830
It's structured,
but there's a lot

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:03.360
of variation to that structure.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:04.830
So where do I store that data?

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:06.630
Can I put it in a
traditional database?

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:08.490
No, that doesn't work.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:09.750
Can I put it into a warehouse?

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:12.910
No, that doesn't work either.

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:15.810
So you started to see a set
of these sort of NoSQL stores

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:17.970
start to emerge around
two main patterns--

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:23.580
one for very wide databases
in terms of columns,

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:25.530
and then another in terms
of having much more,

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:27.527
sort of, hierarchy
and structure,

00:12:27.527 --> 00:12:29.610
typically for capturing
things like JSON documents

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.235
and other things that
are very familiar

00:12:31.235 --> 00:12:33.720
for mobile applications.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:36.180
And then at the
far right there--

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:37.830
so actually, let
me back up a sec.

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:41.290
So this has caused, frankly,
a lot of confusion, right?

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.489
There's a lot of
new technologies

00:12:43.489 --> 00:12:44.280
that are out there.

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:45.904
There's a lot of
people who are looking

00:12:45.904 --> 00:12:49.931
to learn a lot of new techniques
and a lot of new patterns.

00:12:49.931 --> 00:12:51.930
Not just ruining this in
terms of infrastructure

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.860
that they run themselves,
but also in the cloud.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:13:00.480
And then at the far
end of that, we just

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:04.010
announced earlier
this month Spanner.

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:05.690
If you're wanting
to run databases

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:07.242
at really large
scale, the only way

00:13:07.242 --> 00:13:08.700
you've been able
to do that to date

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:11.910
has just been to scale up and
up and up and up, and buy bigger

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:13.987
and bigger and bigger boxes.

00:13:13.987 --> 00:13:16.320
But with Spanner now, that's
offering a really different

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:19.650
paradigm to really give
you a very effective way

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:24.570
to scale out and actually get
the scale and power of a really

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:29.040
large-scale database with
the simplicity of something

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:29.880
like Spanner.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.410
And along the bottom there-- and
we'll go through these in more

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:32.640
detail--

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:34.620
I've put the set
of Google products

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:37.470
that are available in
GCP that are the products

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:39.647
that sort of help meet
you on that journey.

00:13:39.647 --> 00:13:41.730
So actually, let's just
get another show of hands.

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:46.870
Who has a, sort of, traditional
relational database in a VM?

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:48.190
All right.

00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:49.990
How many people
have data warehouses

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:51.880
that they're dealing with?

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:53.460
All right.

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:56.640
How many people are using
Hadoop and big data?

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:58.230
Wow, we've got
everybody here today.

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.660
How many people have used
a wide column database

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.810
in some shape or form?

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:06.680
OK, how about documents
stores, NoSQL stores?

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.240
All right.

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:11.620
How many people
have used Spanner?

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.660
I know you're from
Google, and [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:16.971 --> 00:14:17.470
Great.

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:18.980
OK, so now we're going
to dive in a little bit

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:20.620
and we'll go through each of
these products in a little bit

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:23.620
more detail and talk about some
of the more specific use cases

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:25.650
that we see for them.

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:29.140
OK, so let's start off
first of all with storage.

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:35.590
So Google Cloud Storage is our
managed object storage service,

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:41.170
so this is for storing, you
know, objects in the cloud.

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:43.700
We offer it in four flavors--

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:47.200
multi-regional, regional,
nearline, and coldline.

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:49.750
Each of those pretty closely
aligns to key use cases

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:53.140
that we see around content
storage and delivery, analytics

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:54.790
and ML, and archival.

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:57.800
And I'll go through those each
in a little bit more detail.

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:00.880
One of the things that we
really love about this product

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:04.450
is that we provide a very
consistent API across all

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:06.130
the different storage classes.

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:08.080
So whether you want
to archive your data

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:09.730
or whether you
want to serve it up

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:13.030
from our most highly
available storage class,

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:15.820
you have the same API
to access that data

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:17.800
and you get the same
performance in terms

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.590
of millisecond latency,
time to first byte,

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:22.720
and in terms of throughput.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.870
So this has actually
let us and our customers

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:28.670
do some really, really
interesting and innovative

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:29.170
things.

00:15:29.170 --> 00:15:33.072
And, of course, we offer this
with a very high availability.

00:15:33.072 --> 00:15:35.530
Now let's go through one of
those use cases in a little bit

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:36.840
more detail.

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:38.320
So content storage and delivery.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:41.530
And, you know, this
is one area where

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:44.650
Google has been very deeply
invested for many years.

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:47.920
As I'm sure you've heard,
Google has several billion user

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:48.820
products.

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:52.400
Many of them require us to serve
content to them really, really,

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:55.720
really quickly, and so
we have an amazing set

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:58.310
of infrastructure in Google
that lets us do that.

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:01.030
And Google Cloud Storage
is only one part of that.

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:05.220
You know, Google Cloud Storage
sits on top of Google's, you

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:09.247
know, world-class
network infrastructure.

00:16:09.247 --> 00:16:11.080
And if you were at the
keynote this morning,

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:13.270
you heard Urs talk a
little bit about that.

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:18.010
We carry a very large portion
of the world's network traffic

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:21.550
on our network, and that lets
us do amazing things like

00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:23.890
invest very deeply
in really important

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:27.160
high-throughput connections,
do really great traffic

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:29.650
optimization, and serve
data really, really well,

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:31.930
and keep it on our
network to the closest

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:34.210
point to our customer.

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:36.690
So this example, content
storage and delivery.

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:38.925
If you take somebody
like, say, I'm

00:16:38.925 --> 00:16:41.500
a company that has media files.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:45.460
Let's say Vimeo--
serves and delivers

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:46.960
media content around the world.

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:48.400
Or you might have
a website where

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:50.020
you want to serve
content, or you just

00:16:50.020 --> 00:16:51.910
have images that
you need to serve

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:54.182
as part of an application.

00:16:54.182 --> 00:16:56.390
So with Google Cloud Storage,
you can store that data

00:16:56.390 --> 00:17:03.190
and object storage and then
tier that data based on how hot

00:17:03.190 --> 00:17:03.760
or not it is.

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:05.718
So typically, you might
store this in something

00:17:05.718 --> 00:17:07.780
like multi-regional storage.

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:10.630
This stores the data
in two regions that

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:13.359
are more than 100
miles apart, so if we

00:17:13.359 --> 00:17:15.880
happen to have a regional
outage for some reason or other,

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:20.319
data will still be served
from within another region

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:22.762
in that area.

00:17:22.762 --> 00:17:24.970
You don't need to change
your application to failover

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:25.886
or anything like that.

00:17:25.886 --> 00:17:27.310
We handle it all transparently.

00:17:27.310 --> 00:17:29.990
And we'll serve content
through directly from that.

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.380
So multi-regional is
a great storage class

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:34.000
for that use case.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:38.110
If you find that your
data is getting cold--

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:41.190
media files are a great
example, or movies

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:42.400
that get uploaded after.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:44.600
And maybe they get used
for, like, a month or two

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:46.570
and they're popular,
but then maybe they

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:48.040
don't get touched for a while.

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:51.010
Well, you know, when
you're storing that data,

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:55.430
you know, that's still data that
people might want to access,

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:59.000
but you can't really take
it offline and put it in,

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.872
like, cheap storage
that's slow to access.

00:18:00.872 --> 00:18:02.330
You still want to
have that ability

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:04.390
to access the data very quickly.

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:05.810
With Google Cloud
Storage, we let

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:07.850
you tier that data through
two storage classes

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:09.700
called nearline
and coldline that

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:11.420
let you basically
pay differently

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:12.759
for how you access the data.

00:18:12.759 --> 00:18:15.050
So you might start out with
the data in multi-regional,

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:18.450
which is the most expensive,
most highly available.

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.930
But then as customers
use the data less,

00:18:20.930 --> 00:18:23.300
you might tier it down or
something like coldline

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:25.280
where you pay much
less for storing it,

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:28.580
and the cost changes a
little bit to pay for access.

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:32.560
So gives a lot of flexibility
for how we do that.

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:35.600
The other great thing that we
do with Google Cloud Storage

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:37.190
is that there's
so much, actually,

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:41.300
that's included just
as part of the product,

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:43.170
it's kind of amazing.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:47.930
Data is edge cache, so if you
store data in the US but you

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:52.010
consume it out of the UK, we
carry that data on our network

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:54.600
through to a POP that's as
close to you as possible--

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:55.826
often your ISP--

00:18:55.826 --> 00:18:56.940
and egress the data there.

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:00.380
It's kind of like basic CDN
functionality for edge caching.

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:02.900
It's kind of built-in, right?

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:04.550
If you want to upload
data, again, we

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:06.560
don't upload over
the public internet.

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:09.920
We upload to our local POP
and then take it from there.

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:12.377
And then again,
there's consistent API

00:19:12.377 --> 00:19:14.210
and consistent performance--
super valuable.

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:15.550
No matter what tier
of storage you're in,

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:16.730
you get the same performance.

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:18.440
The contract we want
with our application

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:20.030
is write your
application, and then

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:21.310
manage your data differently.

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:24.110
Your performance profile
for your application

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:25.930
won't change at all.

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:29.150
So as well as using just GCS
in its, kind of, raw form,

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:32.660
we also have both the cloud
CDN and great partnerships

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:34.610
with other CDN
providers, and these

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:38.750
help you have more
control over the caching

00:19:38.750 --> 00:19:41.210
policies for your data,
where you peer your data,

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:44.630
when it peers, and
really sort of give you

00:19:44.630 --> 00:19:46.160
extra flexibility
in how you build

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:49.059
and deliver applications here.

00:19:49.059 --> 00:19:51.350
You saw this morning, also,
we have a great partnership

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:55.149
with Firebase, one of
our teams here at Google,

00:19:55.149 --> 00:19:57.440
and making it really easy
for you to leverage something

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:00.530
like this for building
mobile applications.

00:20:00.530 --> 00:20:05.100
OK, so that's content storage
and delivery for Cloud Storage.

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:07.550
OK, another use case
for Cloud Storage--

00:20:07.550 --> 00:20:10.610
this is an analytics pipeline.

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:14.120
So here, you might want to
capture large amounts of data

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:18.620
from a number of different
sources for, say, marketing.

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:20.370
Maybe you're an e-commerce site.

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:22.197
You're capturing lots
of data, log data,

00:20:22.197 --> 00:20:23.780
potentially, from
your machines that's

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:27.690
capturing usage of what
people have been doing.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:31.340
You might want to capture
Facebook sentiment, Twitter

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:33.770
feeds, et cetera, so you
might have both data coming

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:36.440
in real time, as well as
data that you're pulling

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:39.500
in occasionally in batch mode.

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:41.120
And really, you
want a single place

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:42.800
to sort of drop all
that data and often

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:43.859
store it very cheaply.

00:20:43.859 --> 00:20:46.400
Maybe you're not quite sure what
you want to do with the data

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:47.900
yet in the future,
and you just need

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:50.480
some place to drop the data
and store it for a long time.

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:51.980
I worked with one
company in the UK,

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:53.750
very large e-commerce site--

00:20:53.750 --> 00:20:56.030
for two years, they spent
two years capturing data,

00:20:56.030 --> 00:20:58.250
and they just dropped it into
the low-cost object storage.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:20:59.870
They weren't sure what they
were going to do with it,

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:01.411
but they knew they
were going to need

00:21:01.411 --> 00:21:03.380
to capture that
object, that raw data,

00:21:03.380 --> 00:21:05.214
so that they could do
their analytics later.

00:21:05.214 --> 00:21:07.046
So if you think you're
going to do analytics

00:21:07.046 --> 00:21:08.540
and ML at some
point in the future,

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:10.910
start thinking about
gathering the data now.

00:21:10.910 --> 00:21:13.220
And a super easy place to
drop and store that data

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:14.600
is somewhere like GCS--

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:15.950
very cheap.

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:19.554
You just pay for the
storage as you use it.

00:21:19.554 --> 00:21:21.470
So something like the
regional storage class--

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:23.136
remember, we have
four storage classes--

00:21:23.136 --> 00:21:25.241
multi-regional, regional,
nearline, coldline.

00:21:25.241 --> 00:21:27.740
The regional storage class is
super interesting for this use

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:29.960
case because what that
does is it will effectively

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:31.700
keep your data within
a single region.

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:33.950
And the reason that's often
important in this use case

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:35.991
is you want to spin up
large amounts of analytics

00:21:35.991 --> 00:21:38.120
and compute to operate
over that data.

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:40.489
Now, sure you could store
the data in multi-regional,

00:21:40.489 --> 00:21:42.530
but then you're going to
pay a latency cost as we

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:44.210
go access that data for you.

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:47.420
So this lets you co-locate
storage and analytics

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:50.154
in a single region,
and then with that, you

00:21:50.154 --> 00:21:52.820
can then start to spin up all of
the other great analytics tools

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:55.470
that we have in
the GCP portfolio.

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:57.380
So if you want to use
Hadoop, take a look

00:21:57.380 --> 00:21:58.800
at something like Dataproc.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:03.350
With Dataproc, you can spin
up a large Hadoop cluster,

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:06.050
pay for it and use it
only for when you need it,

00:22:06.050 --> 00:22:09.020
bind it to the data that's in
GCS, and when you're finished,

00:22:09.020 --> 00:22:09.590
shut it down.

00:22:09.590 --> 00:22:11.630
So that's great for
doing batch analytics.

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:15.560
Cloud Dataflow-- super powerful
set of tools and capabilities

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:18.820
for running both streaming
and batch analytics.

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:20.630
Of course, you can
use machine learning.

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:22.520
Appoint machine
learning to this data.

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:24.830
And then the pattern
that we often see here,

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:26.455
and you might have
heard the term "data

00:22:26.455 --> 00:22:29.690
lake" is use a data lake to kind
of drop all of this raw data,

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:32.840
and then you sort of
transform the data

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:34.580
and rewrite it back
into the data lake,

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:36.800
and you sort of do all
of your batch pipelines

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:38.450
and analytics on that data.

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:39.950
And then when you've
sort of come up

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:42.283
with an interesting data set
that you're ready to serve,

00:22:42.283 --> 00:22:44.840
often that's a great set
of data then take and put

00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:47.750
into something that's a more
structured store for analytics

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:49.520
like BigQuery, right?

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:53.150
So you might do lots or
raw analytics on your data,

00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:55.070
establish some kind
of batch processing,

00:22:55.070 --> 00:23:01.250
and then push that
data into BigQuery

00:23:01.250 --> 00:23:05.210
for serving and accessing
via your favorite BI

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.310
tool of choice.

00:23:07.310 --> 00:23:11.860
So this, again-- another very
powerful pattern that we see.

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:14.210
And, you know, the nice
part, again, about the cloud

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:16.940
is we've done this really
nice job of separating compute

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:17.600
from storage.

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:20.780
So you just pay for the
storage that you need,

00:23:20.780 --> 00:23:23.780
pay for the compute and the
analytics that you need,

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:29.110
and build these very
sophisticated pipelines.

00:23:29.110 --> 00:23:32.375
OK, so if you're also
thinking about hey,

00:23:32.375 --> 00:23:34.500
what about all that data
I've still got on premise?

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:36.680
What am I going
to do about that?

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:38.832
How am I going to
leverage the cloud for it,

00:23:38.832 --> 00:23:40.540
or how am I going to
get it to the cloud?

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:42.580
So we've got a set of
interesting use cases

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:46.030
and a set of partners that
we work with closely here.

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:49.750
If I want to ingest data, set
of partners to help ingest data,

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:53.240
whether that be structured data
or large-scale batch data, disk

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:55.040
shipping, for example.

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:56.730
We have a set of
partners for that.

00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:58.854
Another use case we see is
just doing backup in DR.

00:23:58.854 --> 00:24:01.750
So typically, you
might be using, like,

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:04.700
Veritas or storage use.

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:06.950
It's an application that
you're already familiar with.

00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:08.742
CloudBerry is something
for the SMB market.

00:24:08.742 --> 00:24:10.991
It's an application that
you're already familiar with.

00:24:10.991 --> 00:24:13.840
And under the covers, that
will store data into something

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:18.520
like Google Cloud Storage
as the object store.

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:21.790
Also, tiering-- this is another
super common use case we see.

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:26.200
Folks have invested in some
on-premises infrastructure,

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.630
but they recognize
they have cold data,

00:24:28.630 --> 00:24:31.930
and they don't want to pay for
more infrastructure on premises

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:33.070
to store that cold data.

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:36.172
So using something
like NetApp or EMC,

00:24:36.172 --> 00:24:38.380
they're able to use the
management tools that they're

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:41.340
familiar with today, and
establish a cloud archival

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:45.010
tier to store their
data off in the cloud.

00:24:45.010 --> 00:24:46.889
So we've got a great
set of partnerships

00:24:46.889 --> 00:24:48.430
with a range of
companies here to let

00:24:48.430 --> 00:24:50.410
you use the tools that
you're familiar with

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:54.850
and still get the benefits of an
on-premises environment that's

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:58.870
bridged and hybrid
across to the cloud.

00:24:58.870 --> 00:25:01.910
And I think that's me.

00:25:01.910 --> 00:25:02.410
Dominic.

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:04.891
And we'll do Q&amp;A at
the end, as well.

00:25:04.891 --> 00:25:07.200
DOMINIC PREUSS: Yep.

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:10.430
Our goal is to do about
40, 45 minutes of talking,

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:12.800
and then give sufficient
time at the end for you

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:14.600
guys to ask questions.

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:17.130
So stepping over into
the database world,

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:19.190
I wanted to start by
talking about Cloud SQL.

00:25:19.190 --> 00:25:24.470
So Cloud SQL is our managed
service for third party RDBMSs.

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:27.040
So today, it supports MySQL.

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:30.530
We are incredibly excited
to announce Postgres support

00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:33.500
in Cloud SQL today,
and we'll continue

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:36.099
to add additional
RDBMSs over time.

00:25:36.099 --> 00:25:38.390
So what this means is that
it's a fully managed service

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:42.210
that allows us to manage your
instances on your behalf.

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:44.490
So we're running MySQL,
we're doing backups,

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:48.050
we're doing security updates,
updating the minor versions

00:25:48.050 --> 00:25:50.790
of the software so that you
don't have to worry about that.

00:25:50.790 --> 00:25:54.290
We act as your DBA to
manage that service for you.

00:25:54.290 --> 00:25:57.470
We do automatic backups
and handle failover.

00:25:57.470 --> 00:25:59.642
So we maintain
failover replicas.

00:25:59.642 --> 00:26:01.100
You as the customer
have the option

00:26:01.100 --> 00:26:03.950
of running a single instance
or adding a failover

00:26:03.950 --> 00:26:06.090
replica for your database.

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:08.600
If you have a failover
replica for your database

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:10.940
and are running in
high-availability mode,

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:17.000
then you have a 99.95%
availability SLA.

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:19.220
One of the things I do
like to point out to people

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:21.530
is that we at Google
very much believe

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:24.537
in putting numbers and
guarantees behind our products.

00:26:24.537 --> 00:26:26.870
So you'll see as we go through
this, all of our products

00:26:26.870 --> 00:26:28.850
have service-level
agreements that

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:31.550
give your expectations of
the availability you'll

00:26:31.550 --> 00:26:32.900
get from that service.

00:26:32.900 --> 00:26:35.066
And it's something that we
feel very strongly about.

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:39.710
So what is a common use case
and a common architecture

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:40.880
for Cloud SQL?

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:42.140
One is e-commerce.

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:44.750
So in this particular
scenario, you

00:26:44.750 --> 00:26:47.044
will have an
e-commerce application.

00:26:47.044 --> 00:26:48.460
It might be running
in App Engine,

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:51.500
it might be running
on Compute Engine.

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:53.820
So this could be WordPress,
it could be Drupal.

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:55.340
You could be running Hybris--

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:57.298
any of one of your various
e-commerce solutions

00:26:57.298 --> 00:27:00.484
that requires a MySQL
or Postgres database.

00:27:00.484 --> 00:27:02.150
In the background,
because we're running

00:27:02.150 --> 00:27:04.880
in high-availability mode,
we have a failover replica

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:09.740
which allows us to failover
in the event of a failure

00:27:09.740 --> 00:27:11.420
of the master node.

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:13.700
We also have added
read replicas.

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:15.800
So one of the reasons
to add read replicas

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:19.230
to the architecture is you
want to either shard your reads

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:20.060
and writes.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:21.950
You might be getting
close to the capacity

00:27:21.950 --> 00:27:23.840
that you can put through
your master node,

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:27.200
so you want to start setting
your reads to a read replica

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.900
so that you get more capacity
for writes on the master.

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:31.730
Another very common
reason to do this

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:33.590
is your analytics workloads.

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:35.840
So you don't want
your analytics queries

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:37.560
that are coming
from your BI tool,

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:40.190
whether it be Tableau,
Looker, or iCharts,

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:43.220
several of the partners
that we have for Cloud SQL--

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:45.800
you don't want the queries
coming from those dashboards

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:48.740
to interfere with your
customers' experience.

00:27:48.740 --> 00:27:53.477
You don't want analytics queries
to stack up and or increase

00:27:53.477 --> 00:27:55.310
the latency that your
outside customers are.

00:27:55.310 --> 00:27:57.590
So quite often, people
will set up a read replica

00:27:57.590 --> 00:28:00.685
and point their BI tools
at this read replica

00:28:00.685 --> 00:28:02.810
to make sure that they've
separated their analytics

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:06.120
workloads and their
production workloads.

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:09.480
So that's a very common way that
we see people using Cloud SQL.

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:11.400
Obviously, with
Postgres, very popular

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:12.847
for mobile applications.

00:28:12.847 --> 00:28:14.430
So we're expecting
to see an increased

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:16.763
number of mobile applications
running Postgres and using

00:28:16.763 --> 00:28:19.670
PostGIS as the Postgres
feature rolls out.

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:25.390
The next one is Cloud Datastore.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:27.970
So Cloud Datastore is our
managed document database

00:28:27.970 --> 00:28:28.850
service.

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:31.120
So if you were building
a mobile or web app that

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:33.610
is consuming and
producing lots of JSON

00:28:33.610 --> 00:28:36.220
or your data is in
a document format,

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:38.990
then Cloud Datastore is a
fantastic solution for you.

00:28:38.990 --> 00:28:42.820
It's optimized for those
mobile and web scenarios.

00:28:42.820 --> 00:28:45.530
It does autoscaling
and autoreplication,

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:48.750
so you can start at zero, just
start throwing data at it,

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:50.380
and it will grow
with your business.

00:28:50.380 --> 00:28:52.070
You only pay for what you use.

00:28:52.070 --> 00:28:55.450
So you pay for write ops
and read ops and storage,

00:28:55.450 --> 00:28:56.950
and you don't pay
for anything else.

00:28:56.950 --> 00:28:59.690
You don't have to worry about
provisioning capacity up front.

00:28:59.690 --> 00:29:02.645
You don't have to worry about
am I going to have 10 users,

00:29:02.645 --> 00:29:04.270
am I going to have
1,000 users, or am I

00:29:04.270 --> 00:29:05.860
going to have 10 million users?

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:07.407
Am I going to become Snapchat?

00:29:07.407 --> 00:29:09.490
You don't have to worry
about any of those things.

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:11.490
You basically just
throwing data at Datastore

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:13.410
and it'll grow
with your service.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:15.760
And again, going back to the
service-level agreements,

00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:19.596
it comes with a
99.95% availability.

00:29:19.596 --> 00:29:21.220
We do offer a regional
version of this,

00:29:21.220 --> 00:29:22.560
which is not multi-region.

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:25.210
So Cloud Datastore by
default is multi-region.

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:26.980
If you use our regional
Cloud Datastore,

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:29.277
then you'll get three
nines of availability.

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:36.480
Today, Cloud Datastore has
lots of success stories

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:38.190
in the mobile gaming space.

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:40.800
You've heard many
times about Pokemon Go.

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:42.750
We've talked about Snapchat.

00:29:42.750 --> 00:29:45.660
But lots of mobile applications
use Cloud Datastore

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:48.120
as its application database.

00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:52.510
So you often have the
application running.

00:29:52.510 --> 00:29:54.090
This could be the
player front end

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:57.510
that has all of the [INAUDIBLE]
about an individual player.

00:29:57.510 --> 00:30:00.870
All of that data is being
stored in Cloud Datastore.

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:04.770
On the back end, you've got your
world view or the world data,

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:06.307
so where are the players?

00:30:06.307 --> 00:30:07.140
What are they doing?

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:08.370
What's going on in the world?

00:30:08.370 --> 00:30:11.040
All that information is running
on a different compute stack.

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:12.540
And again, all of
that data is being

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:13.830
stored in Cloud Datastore.

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:15.727
You don't have
multiple databases,

00:30:15.727 --> 00:30:17.310
you aren't running
multiple instances.

00:30:17.310 --> 00:30:19.935
You're putting all of your data
into a single data store, which

00:30:19.935 --> 00:30:23.910
simplifies your architecture
and makes it easy to both deploy

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:27.300
and scale up your application.

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:31.230
On the front end, you're using
Google Cloud Load Balancer.

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:33.694
We don't talk about this as
much as we probably should,

00:30:33.694 --> 00:30:35.110
but the Google
Cloud Load Balancer

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:39.210
is actually a fantastic product
because it isn't a single VM

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:40.680
being spun up in your project.

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:43.374
It is a global load balancer
that happens at the edge.

00:30:43.374 --> 00:30:44.790
And so what that
means is that you

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:47.640
don't have regional dependencies
on your load balancer,

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:50.170
and it allows you to load
balance across regions.

00:30:50.170 --> 00:30:52.350
So in this particular
scenario, this application

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:56.310
may be a GKE cluster
running in multiple regions

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:59.490
all speaking to the same
Cloud Datastore back end.

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:02.340
And you can load balance
globally between those regions

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:05.433
because the Google Cloud Load
Balancer is a global service.

00:31:11.110 --> 00:31:13.220
We talked a little
bit about Firebase.

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:16.620
We're very excited to continue
to leverage the amazing work

00:31:16.620 --> 00:31:19.200
that the Firebase team
has done, and to provide

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:22.220
a first-rate mobile developer
experience for Google Cloud

00:31:22.220 --> 00:31:22.916
Platform.

00:31:22.916 --> 00:31:25.040
If you're a mobile developer
coming to Google Cloud

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:28.640
Platform, Firebase is really
that optimized experience

00:31:28.640 --> 00:31:31.280
for you to build your
applications on top of.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:34.310
They do provide a real
time database option.

00:31:34.310 --> 00:31:37.400
So this is a managed
mobile database service.

00:31:37.400 --> 00:31:40.610
The real strength of
Firebase as a platform

00:31:40.610 --> 00:31:44.420
is the number of services
that it provides, but also

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:46.310
the native SDKs.

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:50.890
So they provide an iOS
SDK, an SDK for Android,

00:31:50.890 --> 00:31:53.510
an SDK for client-side
JavaScript.

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:55.340
So if you're building
an application

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:59.060
on multiple platforms, Firebase
gives you a unified platform

00:31:59.060 --> 00:32:00.712
for building your applications.

00:32:00.712 --> 00:32:03.170
The other thing that's very
important where building mobile

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:04.520
is offline access.

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:07.820
So if your customer is using
your application and they are

00:32:07.820 --> 00:32:10.430
not connected to the internet--
they might be in a tunnel,

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:12.830
they might be in the subway,
they might be in the middle

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:15.410
of the woods in Yosemite
and they don't have internet

00:32:15.410 --> 00:32:16.220
access--

00:32:16.220 --> 00:32:19.100
you still have access to all
of your database information

00:32:19.100 --> 00:32:21.890
because there's an offline
version of the data.

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:24.826
And then when that mobile
device comes back online,

00:32:24.826 --> 00:32:26.450
it handles synchronization
to make sure

00:32:26.450 --> 00:32:28.460
that the data on the device
is synced with the data that's

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:29.330
in the cloud.

00:32:29.330 --> 00:32:34.766
So a very powerful application
from the Firebase folks.

00:32:34.766 --> 00:32:36.140
And again, the
beauty of Firebase

00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:38.230
is because you're
building on their SDKs,

00:32:38.230 --> 00:32:39.810
your architecture
is very simple.

00:32:39.810 --> 00:32:41.480
You have a number
of devices that

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:44.670
have integrated their SDKs
into your application,

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:46.880
and it's all going to
a centralized location

00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:48.440
in the back.

00:32:48.440 --> 00:32:51.950
A good example here is
Firebase realtime database.

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:54.260
Another good example
is Firebase Analytics.

00:32:54.260 --> 00:32:56.510
You might be using Firebase
Analytics integrated

00:32:56.510 --> 00:32:58.010
in all of your
apps, and then it's

00:32:58.010 --> 00:33:00.860
very easy to export that
data from Firebase Analytics

00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:04.170
into BigQuery so that you
can run your ad hoc queries.

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:05.990
So because we have
this tight integration

00:33:05.990 --> 00:33:09.620
between the Firebase services
and the Google Cloud Platform

00:33:09.620 --> 00:33:12.652
services, it makes it very
easy for you to leverage one,

00:33:12.652 --> 00:33:15.110
and then once you need to get
the power of the Google Cloud

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:18.160
Platform, it's trivial to move
your data over and move it

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:20.410
into some of the other Google
Cloud Platform services.

00:33:24.140 --> 00:33:25.930
Next is Cloud Bigtable.

00:33:25.930 --> 00:33:29.460
So Cloud Bigtable is our managed
analytics database service.

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:33.350
So this is a managed wide
column database service.

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:37.190
We at Google are firmly
committed to the idea

00:33:37.190 --> 00:33:38.540
of being the open cloud.

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:40.160
One of the best
examples of this is

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:43.970
that we took our internal
Bigtable technology

00:33:43.970 --> 00:33:46.220
and externalized it
to the outside world

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:48.710
as an HBase-compatible
client library.

00:33:48.710 --> 00:33:50.330
We wanted the
outside world to be

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:54.080
able to adopt the Bigtable
technology in the easiest way

00:33:54.080 --> 00:33:56.040
possible with the least
amount of friction.

00:33:56.040 --> 00:33:58.560
And we found that by giving
them an HBase-compatible client

00:33:58.560 --> 00:34:02.180
library, it allowed them to
very seamlessly move their HBase

00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:05.600
clusters over and copy their
data from one HBase cluster

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:09.020
into ours, and therefore moving
from a self-managed solution

00:34:09.020 --> 00:34:11.780
that could be running on
premise or on Compute Engine

00:34:11.780 --> 00:34:16.280
VMs onto a fully managed
database service.

00:34:16.280 --> 00:34:18.139
The power of Cloud
Bigtable is that it

00:34:18.139 --> 00:34:20.159
gives you very low-latency
reads and writes,

00:34:20.159 --> 00:34:23.090
so you get single-millisecond
reads and writes, and gives you

00:34:23.090 --> 00:34:25.730
very fast table scans
so that you can quickly

00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:28.850
scan through your data and
pull out maybe the most

00:34:28.850 --> 00:34:33.000
recent version of a metric,
or do these very large table

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:34.460
scans.

00:34:34.460 --> 00:34:37.429
Currently, Cloud Bigtable
is a zonal service,

00:34:37.429 --> 00:34:40.936
and so it offers you
99.5% availability

00:34:40.936 --> 00:34:41.810
for all of your data.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:47.420
A very common use case that
we see for Cloud Bigtable

00:34:47.420 --> 00:34:51.082
Internet of Things, so IoT.

00:34:51.082 --> 00:34:53.290
What we often see is that
we have a number of devices

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:55.449
generating information.

00:34:55.449 --> 00:34:58.090
From Dave's example earlier, it
might be a Rolls Royce engine

00:34:58.090 --> 00:35:00.610
developing telemetry,
sending telemetry back

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:02.350
to your centralized location.

00:35:02.350 --> 00:35:05.860
It might be sensors that are
sending back sensor data.

00:35:05.860 --> 00:35:09.130
It might be mobile
devices sending back

00:35:09.130 --> 00:35:12.850
event data or things that
are happening on the device.

00:35:12.850 --> 00:35:14.410
Basically, you
have a large number

00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:17.890
of devices generating a large
number of pieces of data

00:35:17.890 --> 00:35:20.350
that all need to be pushed
back into Google Cloud Platform

00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:23.710
to be analyzed maybe
today, maybe in the future,

00:35:23.710 --> 00:35:25.960
depending on what
you're trying to do.

00:35:25.960 --> 00:35:27.774
So the way that we do
this consolidation,

00:35:27.774 --> 00:35:30.190
and one of the best ways to
get data into the Google Cloud

00:35:30.190 --> 00:35:32.350
Platform, is through Pub/Sub.

00:35:32.350 --> 00:35:36.010
So Cloud Pub/Sub is our
message queue product.

00:35:36.010 --> 00:35:39.320
Similar to our load balancer,
it is a global service.

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:42.910
So if you have mobile devices or
sensors or Rolls Royce engines

00:35:42.910 --> 00:35:45.220
around the world that all
need to be able to push back

00:35:45.220 --> 00:35:47.470
into a centralized
location, Cloud Pub/Sub

00:35:47.470 --> 00:35:50.211
gives you a global service to
be able to push that data back.

00:35:50.211 --> 00:35:52.210
You don't have to worry
that that device happens

00:35:52.210 --> 00:35:54.820
to be in Asia and your service
is in the United States.

00:35:54.820 --> 00:35:57.220
You're just pushing
it into Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:35:57.220 --> 00:35:58.870
and you will enter
the Google network

00:35:58.870 --> 00:36:00.280
at the closest
possible location,

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:02.860
and that data will be
stored in Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:36:02.860 --> 00:36:05.650
We then offer two different
ways of getting the data out

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:07.690
of Cloud Pub/Sub, getting
all those messages

00:36:07.690 --> 00:36:08.710
into your system.

00:36:08.710 --> 00:36:13.510
So Cloud Dataproc is our managed
open source data processing

00:36:13.510 --> 00:36:17.080
platform that supports
MapReduce and SPARC.

00:36:17.080 --> 00:36:19.570
So if you have SPARC jobs that
you're running on premise,

00:36:19.570 --> 00:36:21.310
you can put them in
the Cloud Dataproc.

00:36:21.310 --> 00:36:23.290
Same thing for MapReduce.

00:36:23.290 --> 00:36:24.340
You can do this in batch.

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:26.180
You can also do
stream with SPARC,

00:36:26.180 --> 00:36:27.924
but most frequently,
we see batch.

00:36:27.924 --> 00:36:30.340
The other one is our fully
managed data processing product

00:36:30.340 --> 00:36:32.380
which is called
Dataflow, which allows

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:35.830
you to build on Apache, Beam.

00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:37.990
So Dataflow is our data
processing framework

00:36:37.990 --> 00:36:40.420
that we just pushed
into the Apache project.

00:36:40.420 --> 00:36:42.730
It was recently accepted
as a top-level Apache

00:36:42.730 --> 00:36:44.800
project called Apache Beam.

00:36:44.800 --> 00:36:48.670
And Apache Beam allows you
to build these portable data

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:52.480
processing pipelines, and is
another very common use case.

00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:54.850
So once you have these
data processing tools

00:36:54.850 --> 00:36:56.350
picking up the data
out of Pub/Sub,

00:36:56.350 --> 00:36:59.110
you're dropping them
into Cloud Bigtable.

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:02.280
So Cloud Bigtable then allows
you to analyze the data.

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:05.680
So it really excels
at time series data.

00:37:05.680 --> 00:37:08.960
We use it quite a bit
for graph use cases.

00:37:08.960 --> 00:37:11.366
And it is very
efficient for writing

00:37:11.366 --> 00:37:13.240
lots and lots and lots
of data and then doing

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:15.040
scans across that data.

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:18.190
It also provides mutations, so
you can do asset transactions

00:37:18.190 --> 00:37:21.190
at a row level if you need to
do a large number of updates,

00:37:21.190 --> 00:37:22.860
as well.

00:37:22.860 --> 00:37:25.690
And so until now, the way that
people would analyze that data,

00:37:25.690 --> 00:37:27.340
they would go and
run these queries.

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:31.720
We're very excited, as well,
to announce at this conference

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:35.020
the ability for BigQuery
federated queries

00:37:35.020 --> 00:37:37.030
to search Bigtable data.

00:37:37.030 --> 00:37:39.400
So what this means is you
can write all of your data

00:37:39.400 --> 00:37:40.254
into Bigtable.

00:37:40.254 --> 00:37:42.670
So if you have a use case that
requires a lot of mutations

00:37:42.670 --> 00:37:45.310
or you need single-millisecond
reads and writes

00:37:45.310 --> 00:37:46.980
where BigQuery
doesn't meet that need

00:37:46.980 --> 00:37:49.702
and you need a database as
opposed to way data warehouse,

00:37:49.702 --> 00:37:51.910
you can write all of that
data in the Cloud Bigtable,

00:37:51.910 --> 00:37:54.340
and now you can run
your SQL queries

00:37:54.340 --> 00:37:58.000
from BigQuery using federated
queries to query the data

00:37:58.000 --> 00:38:01.980
inline without copying it or
e-tailing it into BigQuery

00:38:01.980 --> 00:38:03.190
in Cloud Bigtable.

00:38:03.190 --> 00:38:05.890
So now we've provided
a native SQL interface

00:38:05.890 --> 00:38:09.270
for doing SQL queries against
your data in Cloud Bigtable.

00:38:13.170 --> 00:38:15.270
The added benefit of
the Cloud Bigtable

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:17.754
federated queries is that any
of our BI tools, or business

00:38:17.754 --> 00:38:19.920
intelligence tools, or data
visualization tools that

00:38:19.920 --> 00:38:22.680
are integrated with BigQuery
now work on top of Bigtable,

00:38:22.680 --> 00:38:23.440
as well.

00:38:23.440 --> 00:38:26.520
So if you are using Tableau
or Looker or iCharts

00:38:26.520 --> 00:38:29.500
or MicroStrategy-- whatever
your firm happens to use,

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:34.400
you now have compatibility with
your data in Cloud Bigtable.

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:38.050
Cloud Spanner-- so we're
able to spend some time on it

00:38:38.050 --> 00:38:40.141
on the keynote today,
and really showed,

00:38:40.141 --> 00:38:41.890
kind of, the scale and
the types of things

00:38:41.890 --> 00:38:43.300
you could do with Spanner.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:46.100
It is our managed
relational database service,

00:38:46.100 --> 00:38:48.370
so it is a horizontally
scalable relational

00:38:48.370 --> 00:38:52.610
database with global consistency
and asset transactions.

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:56.900
And so what this means is that
your data can grow with you.

00:38:56.900 --> 00:38:58.994
So if you happen to start
on a smaller data set,

00:38:58.994 --> 00:39:00.410
and as you're going
over time, you

00:39:00.410 --> 00:39:02.840
don't have to worry about
outgrowing your existing

00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:04.100
relational database.

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:08.930
If you're currently
running a sharded MySQL

00:39:08.930 --> 00:39:11.330
or manually sharded
Postgres implementation,

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:14.120
then you know the pain that
comes along with doing that,

00:39:14.120 --> 00:39:17.370
and Spanner starts to
provide real value to you.

00:39:17.370 --> 00:39:19.310
Another example is
high availability.

00:39:19.310 --> 00:39:21.740
So if you're running a
master-slave scenario

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:23.960
with any traditional
RDBMS, you have

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:26.810
to accept downtime on
failover for things

00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:29.810
like software upgrade,
security patches, maintenance.

00:39:29.810 --> 00:39:32.510
Because Spanner
is distributed, we

00:39:32.510 --> 00:39:34.010
don't have to worry about that.

00:39:34.010 --> 00:39:35.690
You don't have
downtime for upgrades.

00:39:35.690 --> 00:39:37.700
We can do all of
the upgrades inline.

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:40.120
You don't have downtime
to do maintenance.

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:43.970
Your service stays up and
is serving your business.

00:39:43.970 --> 00:39:46.007
Because of this
architecture and the fact

00:39:46.007 --> 00:39:47.840
that it's a fundamentally
different approach

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:49.730
to relational
databases, we can offer

00:39:49.730 --> 00:39:52.610
four nines of availability
for our regional instances.

00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:57.620
So that's four minutes and 23
seconds of downtime per month.

00:39:57.620 --> 00:39:59.236
And with the
multi-regional versions

00:39:59.236 --> 00:40:00.860
that we're rolling
out later this year,

00:40:00.860 --> 00:40:02.870
you'll get five nines
of availability.

00:40:02.870 --> 00:40:05.220
That's 26 seconds
of downtime a month.

00:40:08.770 --> 00:40:10.670
So how do people
use Cloud Spanner?

00:40:10.670 --> 00:40:12.420
We've been running an
early access program

00:40:12.420 --> 00:40:14.472
for over a year with
a number of customers,

00:40:14.472 --> 00:40:16.680
and we've already started
to see very strong adoption

00:40:16.680 --> 00:40:18.870
from the Cloud Spanner
product in beta.

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:22.270
One of the very common use
cases is global supply chain.

00:40:22.270 --> 00:40:24.690
So places that you really
care about global consistency

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:28.170
are financial transactions,
inventory management,

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:31.320
places where being off
by one really matters.

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:33.996
If you have five things and
you try to sell six of them,

00:40:33.996 --> 00:40:35.370
then you have an
unhappy customer

00:40:35.370 --> 00:40:37.160
and that's a bad
customer experience.

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:39.780
And so Cloud Spanner
provides you a consistent way

00:40:39.780 --> 00:40:41.230
of storing that data.

00:40:41.230 --> 00:40:44.250
So a common way that we see
this is that in supply chain,

00:40:44.250 --> 00:40:46.320
basically Cloud Datastore
access your ledger

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:48.090
for all of your inventory.

00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:49.590
You might have an
application, which

00:40:49.590 --> 00:40:51.266
is your supply chain,
that is working

00:40:51.266 --> 00:40:52.890
with your various
vendors that happened

00:40:52.890 --> 00:40:57.000
to be delivering
pieces of your product

00:40:57.000 --> 00:40:58.417
and then going
into assembly line.

00:40:58.417 --> 00:41:00.416
And then once those
products have been produced,

00:41:00.416 --> 00:41:02.190
they go into a separate
inventory system.

00:41:02.190 --> 00:41:04.380
So instead of having
multiple data sources where

00:41:04.380 --> 00:41:07.570
you store your supply chain
information and your inventory,

00:41:07.570 --> 00:41:09.540
you can store them all
into a single database

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:11.940
with a single source of
truth so that you always

00:41:11.940 --> 00:41:14.745
have a global view of what's
going on with your inventory

00:41:14.745 --> 00:41:16.985
and your supply chain.

00:41:16.985 --> 00:41:18.610
Another great thing
about Cloud Spanner

00:41:18.610 --> 00:41:20.860
is that it's an application
database that you can also

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:21.910
do analytics on.

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:24.010
And so you can connect
your analytics queries

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:27.400
to Cloud Spanner, to relational
database running SQL.

00:41:27.400 --> 00:41:30.130
We've already announced a
number of launch partners

00:41:30.130 --> 00:41:32.440
for data visualization
and BI tools.

00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:35.090
We'll continue to roll those out
through the rest of the year.

00:41:35.090 --> 00:41:36.881
But you can also run
your analytics queries

00:41:36.881 --> 00:41:37.900
on top of Cloud Spanner.

00:41:37.900 --> 00:41:39.400
And if you need
additional capacity,

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:41.140
you don't have to
create a read replica.

00:41:41.140 --> 00:41:43.850
You just make sure that you're
provisioned appropriately

00:41:43.850 --> 00:41:46.300
for your compute nodes to
be able to handle that load.

00:41:50.140 --> 00:41:51.180
And BigQuery.

00:41:51.180 --> 00:41:52.995
So coming back, there's
lots of sessions

00:41:52.995 --> 00:41:54.370
at the conference
about BigQuery,

00:41:54.370 --> 00:41:57.130
but I want to touch
on it briefly.

00:41:57.130 --> 00:42:00.790
Google BigQuery query is our
cloud-native data warehouse.

00:42:00.790 --> 00:42:03.440
It's a fully managed
analytics data warehouse.

00:42:03.440 --> 00:42:04.900
It gives you tremendous speed.

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:06.316
The thing that we
often talk about

00:42:06.316 --> 00:42:08.200
is speed, which is
that you can scan

00:42:08.200 --> 00:42:10.720
terabytes of data in seconds.

00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:12.850
We also talk about scale.

00:42:12.850 --> 00:42:14.890
Just like all of our
serverless applications,

00:42:14.890 --> 00:42:18.260
you don't have to
provision capacity upfront.

00:42:18.260 --> 00:42:20.890
You don't have to say, I
need a terabyte of storage.

00:42:20.890 --> 00:42:22.336
You say, I have data.

00:42:22.336 --> 00:42:23.710
Here's my data,
and now I'm going

00:42:23.710 --> 00:42:25.060
to start running some queries.

00:42:25.060 --> 00:42:26.601
There's very little
upfront work that

00:42:26.601 --> 00:42:28.360
has to be done except
defining a schema.

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:29.818
You can start
writing data into it,

00:42:29.818 --> 00:42:31.510
you don't have to
provision it, and then

00:42:31.510 --> 00:42:34.450
you can just start
running your app queries.

00:42:34.450 --> 00:42:36.900
And BigQuery,
again-- big on SLAs.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:44.320
BigQuery offers a
99.9% availability SLA.

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:46.045
What is a common
reference architecture?

00:42:46.045 --> 00:42:47.420
Kind of going back
to our example

00:42:47.420 --> 00:42:50.740
before, very similar
architecture-- writing

00:42:50.740 --> 00:42:54.396
a bunch of logs data
into Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:42:54.396 --> 00:42:56.020
So again, this might
be mobile devices,

00:42:56.020 --> 00:42:57.630
it might be IoT devices.

00:42:57.630 --> 00:43:00.204
It might be applications
that are generating events.

00:43:00.204 --> 00:43:02.370
All those events get written
into the Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:43:02.370 --> 00:43:04.990
You process them with the
data processing pipelines

00:43:04.990 --> 00:43:06.760
and drop them into BigQuery.

00:43:06.760 --> 00:43:09.670
Now over time, you can go back
and run historical queries.

00:43:09.670 --> 00:43:12.130
You can see how often are those
events happening over time.

00:43:12.130 --> 00:43:15.070
You get auto date
partitioning of your tables

00:43:15.070 --> 00:43:17.710
so that you can go back
and run those queries

00:43:17.710 --> 00:43:19.030
in an efficient way.

00:43:19.030 --> 00:43:21.611
And again, because
it uses that same SQL

00:43:21.611 --> 00:43:23.110
and you get that
same interface, you

00:43:23.110 --> 00:43:26.290
get the same BI tools like
Tableau, Looker, and iCharts.

00:43:29.360 --> 00:43:29.860
Great.

00:43:29.860 --> 00:43:32.650
So Dave and I have just
gone through a large number

00:43:32.650 --> 00:43:34.450
of products.

00:43:34.450 --> 00:43:36.220
Hopefully we have
a managed service

00:43:36.220 --> 00:43:37.900
to meet every one
of your use cases.

00:43:37.900 --> 00:43:39.359
Obviously, if there
isn't one here,

00:43:39.359 --> 00:43:41.691
we have lots of tools to help
you run your own workloads

00:43:41.691 --> 00:43:43.237
on Google Cloud
Platform, but we want

00:43:43.237 --> 00:43:45.070
to make sure that we
have at least a managed

00:43:45.070 --> 00:43:47.050
service for each
of your workloads

00:43:47.050 --> 00:43:49.420
so that if you choose to go
to the managed service path,

00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:50.870
we have an option for you.

00:43:50.870 --> 00:43:54.107
So how do I decide?

00:43:54.107 --> 00:43:55.940
So here's a quick
overview of all the things

00:43:55.940 --> 00:43:57.620
we've talked about today.

00:43:57.620 --> 00:43:59.480
We left out
persistent disk, which

00:43:59.480 --> 00:44:02.150
we can talk about, which
is our block source device,

00:44:02.150 --> 00:44:05.034
Firebase because it's
just too big a slide.

00:44:05.034 --> 00:44:07.200
But effectively, this takes
you through our options.

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:08.750
So if you were
looking for in-memory,

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:10.460
we offer App Engine memcache.

00:44:10.460 --> 00:44:12.410
So if you have an App
Engine application that

00:44:12.410 --> 00:44:13.910
requires memcache
for caching, we

00:44:13.910 --> 00:44:15.890
provide that as a
managed service.

00:44:15.890 --> 00:44:19.650
If you're looking for managed
MySQL or managed Postgres--

00:44:19.650 --> 00:44:22.250
I'm so happy to be
able to say that now--

00:44:22.250 --> 00:44:25.470
you have Cloud SQL for
running your databases.

00:44:25.470 --> 00:44:28.910
If you outgrow what you can
do in MySQL or Postgres,

00:44:28.910 --> 00:44:30.980
or need the super high
availability that you

00:44:30.980 --> 00:44:32.600
can get from Cloud
Spanner, then you

00:44:32.600 --> 00:44:35.120
can move up to Cloud Spanner
as your scale-out and highly

00:44:35.120 --> 00:44:39.042
available database for your
mission-critical applications.

00:44:39.042 --> 00:44:41.000
If you're building a
mobile and web application

00:44:41.000 --> 00:44:45.680
and your data is document-based,
then Cloud Datastore

00:44:45.680 --> 00:44:50.500
gives you a very easy onboard
to go from zero to petabytes

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:54.860
of scale with that
data structure

00:44:54.860 --> 00:44:57.220
and a very easy method for
getting up and running.

00:44:57.220 --> 00:44:59.660
Cloud Bigtable is our
analytics database.

00:44:59.660 --> 00:45:02.330
So if you have key-value
data or wide column data

00:45:02.330 --> 00:45:05.330
that requires lots of
mutations, Cloud Bigtable.

00:45:05.330 --> 00:45:08.370
Cloud storage-- another use case
that we don't really talk about

00:45:08.370 --> 00:45:10.370
and why I always like to
include it in the slide

00:45:10.370 --> 00:45:13.820
is because BigQuery has
federated queries, and the fact

00:45:13.820 --> 00:45:17.570
that BigQuery can search
BigQuery columnar data,

00:45:17.570 --> 00:45:21.580
it can search G Drive files, but
it can also search GCS files.

00:45:21.580 --> 00:45:25.040
So another common pattern is
customers writing Avro files

00:45:25.040 --> 00:45:26.840
into a GCS bucket
and then running

00:45:26.840 --> 00:45:31.170
BigQuery to run their SQL
queries all over that data.

00:45:31.170 --> 00:45:32.754
So unlike some of
the other providers,

00:45:32.754 --> 00:45:34.669
we don't think that
that's a separate product,

00:45:34.669 --> 00:45:36.710
we just think that that's
a feature of BigQuery.

00:45:36.710 --> 00:45:38.501
And we see that pattern
over and over again

00:45:38.501 --> 00:45:41.590
as a way for customers to
maintain that data in a GCS

00:45:41.590 --> 00:45:43.010
bucket and to run those queries.

00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:47.770
So how do I decide?

00:45:47.770 --> 00:45:48.730
The magic slide.

00:45:48.730 --> 00:45:50.112
Left it for the end.

00:45:50.112 --> 00:45:52.570
So people tell me time and time
again, please, just tell me

00:45:52.570 --> 00:45:54.460
what product to
use, so I've tried

00:45:54.460 --> 00:45:56.200
to take the last
two years of advice

00:45:56.200 --> 00:45:58.360
and put it into a
single flow chart.

00:45:58.360 --> 00:46:02.800
And what this flowchart says is,
great, is your data structured?

00:46:02.800 --> 00:46:05.200
If the answer is no,
then the next question

00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:06.880
is do you need mobile SDKs?

00:46:06.880 --> 00:46:08.770
If you need a mobile
SDK, then please

00:46:08.770 --> 00:46:11.320
use Cloud Storage for Firebase.

00:46:11.320 --> 00:46:13.150
If you don't need
mobile SDKs, then you

00:46:13.150 --> 00:46:14.950
can use Cloud Storage directly.

00:46:14.950 --> 00:46:16.610
All the data goes
into the same place,

00:46:16.610 --> 00:46:19.390
it's just two different ways of
getting data into the system.

00:46:19.390 --> 00:46:21.520
If your data is structured,
then the question

00:46:21.520 --> 00:46:23.680
is is this an
analytics workload?

00:46:23.680 --> 00:46:26.300
Are you trying to run analytics?

00:46:26.300 --> 00:46:28.610
Is the application
latency-sensitive?

00:46:28.610 --> 00:46:31.270
Are you going to run back
over historical data?

00:46:31.270 --> 00:46:33.400
If the answer is yes,
then the next question

00:46:33.400 --> 00:46:36.370
is do you need updates
or low latency?

00:46:36.370 --> 00:46:38.620
So BigQuery offers
you mutations,

00:46:38.620 --> 00:46:40.990
but there's a limit as
to how many you can do.

00:46:40.990 --> 00:46:43.450
So if you have to do a
high volume of mutations

00:46:43.450 --> 00:46:47.740
or you need really
low-latency reads and writes,

00:46:47.740 --> 00:46:49.150
then you go to Cloud Bigtable.

00:46:49.150 --> 00:46:50.566
If you don't need
either of those,

00:46:50.566 --> 00:46:53.140
then you can go to BigQuery,
and BigQuery will provide you

00:46:53.140 --> 00:46:55.270
all of the analytics
functions in SQL,

00:46:55.270 --> 00:46:57.790
historical, autopartitioning,
and all of the things

00:46:57.790 --> 00:47:00.680
that BigQuery does
so amazingly well.

00:47:00.680 --> 00:47:02.600
So if you are not
building an application,

00:47:02.600 --> 00:47:05.450
an analytics workload, so now
you're in this main part here.

00:47:05.450 --> 00:47:07.670
So then the question is
is your data relational?

00:47:07.670 --> 00:47:11.720
If your data model is
inherently non-relational,

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:14.210
then the next question is
do you need mobile SDKs?

00:47:14.210 --> 00:47:16.820
If you need mobile SDKs and
things like offline access,

00:47:16.820 --> 00:47:19.227
then you go to the
Firebase realtime database.

00:47:19.227 --> 00:47:21.560
If you don't, and you need
client-side client libraries,

00:47:21.560 --> 00:47:24.610
then you go to
Claudie Datastore.

00:47:24.610 --> 00:47:27.050
If your data is relational
and the answer is yes,

00:47:27.050 --> 00:47:29.740
then the next question is do
you need horizontal scalability?

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:33.610
If you don't need horizontal
scalability or the incredibly

00:47:33.610 --> 00:47:35.550
high availability that
comes along with that,

00:47:35.550 --> 00:47:37.660
then Cloud SQL, either
MySQL or Postgres,

00:47:37.660 --> 00:47:39.880
are going to give you
the best experience

00:47:39.880 --> 00:47:41.800
for getting your
application up and running.

00:47:41.800 --> 00:47:43.960
Incredibly strong
ecosystem, tools

00:47:43.960 --> 00:47:47.770
that integrate with both,
lots of community-- developer

00:47:47.770 --> 00:47:49.650
community, developer
knowledge-- around it,

00:47:49.650 --> 00:47:51.190
and so it's a great solution
for getting your application up

00:47:51.190 --> 00:47:52.150
and running.

00:47:52.150 --> 00:47:54.125
If you do need
horizontal scalability,

00:47:54.125 --> 00:47:55.750
then you come to
Cloud Spanner, and you

00:47:55.750 --> 00:47:57.166
can start looking
at Cloud Spanner

00:47:57.166 --> 00:48:00.070
as your horizontally
scalable relational database

00:48:00.070 --> 00:48:02.290
so that you know that
when you need the scale

00:48:02.290 --> 00:48:03.956
and when you need the
high availability,

00:48:03.956 --> 00:48:05.320
it's there for you.

00:48:05.320 --> 00:48:07.150
So with that, Dave
and I would like

00:48:07.150 --> 00:48:09.170
to open it up for questions.

